 "differences": [
  "The files were exported from a spreadsheet and exist in three layouts. In the layout with one value per cell the comma between two lists lies inside one cell; where the spreadsheet had turned that cell into a number, the digits were joined, and they were split again where the neighbouring values continue best (a rule of ours, not the authors'). Rows whose trace could not be rebuilt keep their scalars and have an empty trace: 201 go only, 87 no trace, 15442 ok, 43 stop only, 19 unparsed rows by status.",
  "The participant label inside 1008 files starts with PD although the file is in a healthy-control folder (or the reverse); the folder and file name were used for the group.",
  "The paper's accuracies (93% PD OFF, 93% PD ON, 99% HC S1, 98% HC S2) did not reproduce from the exclude column under the read-me rule (percent of non-practice trials not marked too slow, wrong way or stopped early, mean over participants): HC S1 98.3 (paper 99); HC S2 97.6 (paper 98); PD OFF 94.2 (paper 93); PD ON 94.1 (paper 93). The paper does not define accuracy (Results cite 93/93/99/98% and Methods list four trial exclusions: RT over 1.5 s, counter-clockwise movement, stopping before the STOP cue, no movement initiated). Checked 11-09: the gap is not a parse problem (the exclude column is read cleanly in every row; the rebuilt traces have no backward time and jumps above 300 px in only 15 of about 15,700 segments, mostly at segment starts). Variants tried (add RT over 1500 ms: no change; add missing RT or start RT: PD 93.5/93.4, HC 98.0/97.2; wrong-way plus early only: PD 95.1/94.7, HC 98.8/98.1; pooled instead of per-participant means: same) reproduce neither pair, so accuracy is not used as a check.",
  "23 rows have a stop segment of a single sample (x, y, time) right before the exclude text; the first release read those three numbers as part of the exclude text (so 13 wrong-way or far-from-circle labels were not recognised) and dropped the sample; both are now fixed.",
  "A few rows have a single value where start_RT and stop_completion_time should both be; it is listed as stop_completion_time_ms with a note in trial_info (in most of them it equals the last stop-segment time, which is how the stop completion time is defined), and start_RT is left out."
 ],
